-- title:
--   Lemma 2 — $I(\omega=1\mid\zeta_0(\alpha))\le n\alpha$
-- statement:
--   Let $(X,\nu)$ be a probability space carrying a state $\omega\in\{0,1\}$ and, for each expert $i\in[n]$, an information $\sigma$-algebra $\mathcal G_i$ and a forecast $F_i$ that is $\mathcal G_i$-measurable and a version of $\mathbb P(\omega=1\mid\mathcal G_i)$, i.e. $\nu(\{\omega=1\}\cap E)=\int_E F_i\,d\nu$ for every $E\in\mathcal G_i$. This is an arbitrary information structure $I$ (not necessarily a partial evidence one): in the paper $X=\Omega\times S_1\times\dots\times S_n$, $\mathcal G_i$ is generated by $s_i$ and $F_i=I(\omega=1,s_i)/(I(\omega=1,s_i)+I(\omega=0,s_i))$. For $\alpha>0$ let
--
--   $$
--   \zeta_0(\alpha)=\{\exists i:\ F_i\le\alpha\},\qquad \zeta_1(\alpha)=\{\exists i:\ F_i\ge1-\alpha\}
--   $$
--
--   be the events that some expert's forecast is extreme low, resp. extreme high.
--
--   **Lemma 2.** For every $\alpha>0$,
--
--   $$
--   \nu\bigl(\{\omega=1\}\cap\zeta_0(\alpha)\bigr)\le n\alpha\,\nu\bigl(\zeta_0(\alpha)\bigr),
--   $$
--
--   i.e. $I(\omega=1\mid\zeta_0(\alpha))\le n\alpha$ whenever $\zeta_0(\alpha)$ has positive probability. Conditioned on some expert forecasting at most $\alpha$, the state is $1$ with probability at most $n\alpha$; this is what lets the aggregator forecast safely on periods with an extreme forecast, without learning.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: information structure I ∈ Δ(Ω × S_1 × … × S_n) with F_i = I(ω = 1, s_i)/(I(ω = 1, s_i) + I(ω = 0, s_i)) → any probability space with forecasts F_i that are 𝓖_i-measurable versions of P(ω = 1 | 𝓖_i) for sub-σ-algebras 𝓖_i; reason: the paper's structure is the case 𝓖_i = σ(s_i)
--   - encoding: I(ω = 1 | ζ_0(α)) ≤ nα → ν({ω = 1} ∩ ζ_0(α)) ≤ nα · ν(ζ_0(α)); reason: the conditional probability is undefined when ν(ζ_0(α)) = 0; the product form is the same inequality otherwise
-- source:
--   Babichenko, Garber, Learning of Optimal Forecast Aggregation in Partial Evidence Environments, arXiv:1802.07107v1, Lemma 2, p.20; proof Appendix A, pp.27–28

import Mathlib

namespace ForecastAggLearn

open MeasureTheory

/-- **Lemma 2: an extreme low forecast caps the probability of ω = 1.**
Babichenko, Garber, arXiv:1802.07107v1, Lemma 2, p.20: for an arbitrary information structure
`I ∈ Δ(Ω × S_1 × … × S_n)` and `ζ_0(α)`, the event that at least one expert's forecast is in
`[0, α]`, "For every α > 0 and every information structure I, I(ω = 1 | ζ_0(α)) ≤ nα."

**Formalization Note.** The information structure is a probability space `(X, ν)` with the state
`ω : X → Bool` and, for each expert `i`, the information `𝓖 i` (a sub-σ-algebra) and the forecast
`F i`, a `𝓖 i`-measurable version of `P(ω = 1 | 𝓖 i)`, i.e. `ν({ω = 1} ∩ E) = ∫_E F_i dν` for every
`E ∈ 𝓖 i`. The paper's `Ω × S_1 × … × S_n` with `F_i = I(ω = 1, s_i)/(I(ω = 1, s_i) + I(ω = 0, s_i))`
is the case `X = Ω × Π_i S_i`, `𝓖 i` generated by `s_i`. The conditional probability, undefined
when `ν(ζ_0(α)) = 0`, is stated in product form `ν({ω = 1} ∩ ζ_0) ≤ nα · ν(ζ_0)`. -/
theorem lemma_2 {X : Type*} [mX : MeasurableSpace X] (ν : Measure X) [IsProbabilityMeasure ν]
    (ω : X → Bool) (hω : Measurable ω) {n : ℕ} (𝓖 : Fin n → MeasurableSpace X)
    (h𝓖 : ∀ i, 𝓖 i ≤ mX) (F : Fin n → X → ℝ) (hFm : ∀ i, Measurable[𝓖 i] (F i))
    (hF : ∀ i, ∀ E, MeasurableSet[𝓖 i] E →
      ν ({x | ω x = true} ∩ E) = ∫⁻ x in E, ENNReal.ofReal (F i x) ∂ν)
    (α : ℝ) (hα : 0 < α) :
    ν ({x | ω x = true} ∩ {x | ∃ i, F i x ≤ α}) ≤
      ENNReal.ofReal (n * α) * ν {x | ∃ i, F i x ≤ α} := by sorry

end ForecastAggLearn
